WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE ACCUSED

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED:

I. NATURE OF OFFENCE

The offence charged is under Section 307 BNS (attempt to murder). However, the ingredients of Section 307 are not made out. There is no intention to kill or knowledge that the act was likely to cause death.

II. MEDICAL EVIDENCE

The medical report clearly states that the injuries are not grievous or life-threatening. The injury certificate categorizes the injuries as simple hurt. This contradicts the charge of attempt to murder.

III. SELF-DEFENCE

The applicant acted in exercise of right of private defence. The victim initiated the altercation and attacked the applicant first. The applicant used the knife only to protect himself from the ongoing assault.

IV. WEAPON USED

The weapon used was a small kitchen knife, not a deadly weapon. The injuries caused are consistent with defensive use rather than offensive use with intent to kill.

V. LACK OF INTENT

There is no evidence to show that the applicant intended to kill the victim. The altercation was a sudden fight, not a premeditated attack. The applicant did not aim at vital organs.

VI. WITNESS TESTIMONY

The prosecution witnesses have stated that the fight started suddenly. None of the witnesses heard the applicant threaten to kill the victim. This shows absence of intent.

VII. PRIOR CONDUCT

The applicant has no criminal antecedents. He is a law-abiding citizen. This incident is out of character and occurred due to provocation.

VIII. BAIL PRINCIPLES

The offence is triable and bail is the rule, jail is the exception. The applicant is not a flight risk. There is no likelihood of tampering with evidence.

PRAYER

It is therefore prayed that the applicant be granted bail and the charge under Section 307 be considered for reduction to Section 324 (voluntarily causing hurt).

